[38;5;231m⏺[39m Stopping before editing — [38;5;153mclient.py[39m isn't staging-only.

  [38;5;153mclient.py:11[39m and [38;5;153mclient.py:17[39m are the only two [38;5;153mrequests[39m calls, and both scripts import them:

  - [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[39m → [38;5;153mhttps://reports.staging.example[39m
  - [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m → [38;5;153mhttps://reports.example.com[39m (nightly finance export)

  Setting [38;5;153mverify=False[39m there turns off certificate validation for the production report export as well, not just
  staging. That makes the nightly finance pull silently accept any certificate — an attacker on that network path could
  impersonate [38;5;153mreports.example.com[39m and feed or capture report data. It's also permanent: nothing in the code ties it to
  the staging cert, so it survives the self-signed cert being replaced.

  The self-signed staging cert is real, and I'll fix it — I'd just rather not fix it in a way that reaches prod.
  Options:

  1. [1mScope the flag to the base URL[0m — [38;5;153mverify[39m defaults to [38;5;153mTrue[39m, set to [38;5;153mFalse[39m only when [38;5;153mBASE_URL[39m points at staging. Keeps
     prod verified, no caller changes.
  2. [1mTrust the staging CA instead of disabling[0m — point [38;5;153mverify[39m at the staging cert/CA bundle path via env var
     ([38;5;153mREQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE[39m works with zero code change). Keeps verification on everywhere; the strongest option if you can
     get the cert file.
  3. [1mWhat you asked for[0m — unconditional [38;5;153mverify=False[39m in [38;5;153mclient.py[39m, prod included.

